{"candidates":[{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["The large service has centralized governance to enforce disjoint dependencies, while the decentralized network relies on the independent choices of operators.","Decentralized nodes may have lower correlation in configuration and software stack due to heterogeneous administration."],"overturn_conditions":["Evidence that decentralized networks inherently select for heterogeneous infrastructure.","Evidence that large services cannot internally achieve multi-region, multi-provider redundancy."],"shared_structure":["Both systems provide a function that must survive node failure.","Both systems have redundant components.","Both systems rely on a supply chain of infrastructure that is vulnerable to common-mode failure."],"source_memory_refs":["ba687c5f145e404c53a84549cf366e0b6e0106eec67404c07231357fed09e672"],"transfer_claims":["Resilience against correlated failure depends on breaking shared dependencies, not on the count of redundant components.","Apparent uncorrelation in decentralized networks is fragile and collapses when a shared dependency fails."]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"A large, internally redundant service is more resilient to correlated failure than a decentralized network of small, independent services when the shared common-mode dependencies (e.g., cloud provider outages, DNS failures, SSL certificate expiry) are deliberately internalized and protected against. Decentralization often creates a false sense of independence because the independent operators share hidden dependencies, making the network brittle to correlated failure.","counterconditions":["If the large service cannot internally isolate its redundant components from shared software supply chain attacks.","If the decentralized network is proven to use entirely disjoint infrastructure stacks (no shared cloud, DNS, or OS providers).","If the large service's internal communication overhead creates a fragility that scales superlinearly with size."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Independently operated services naturally converge on a monoculture of underlying infrastructure and libraries (e.g., AWS us-east-1, Cloudflare, shared open-source packages). A large service can identify and eliminate these single points of failure by explicitly engineering internal redundancy (e.g., multi-region deployment, defense-in-depth). Therefore, the decentralized network's independence is merely apparent, while the large service achieves actual resilience by actively breaking correlation.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.7}],"simulation_proposals":[]}